=========================================================
MULTI-LEADER (MULTI-WRITER) REPLICATION — MASTER NOTES
=========================================================

---------------------------------------------------------
0. REAL LIFE INTUITION (VERY IMPORTANT)
---------------------------------------------------------

Example 1: Google Docs (REAL CASE)
- Multiple users editing same document simultaneously
- Everyone can WRITE (no single leader)

Example 2: Git (Distributed Version Control)
- Every developer can commit locally
- Later changes are merged

Example 3: Multi-region apps (Instagram, Netflix)
- US users write to US DB
- India users write to India DB
- Later → data sync across regions

CORE IDEA:
→ Multiple nodes can ACCEPT WRITES
→ Data replicated across all nodes

---------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT IS MULTI-LEADER REPLICATION?
---------------------------------------------------------

DEFINITION:
- Multiple nodes act as LEADERS
- Each leader accepts writes
- Leaders replicate data to each other

---------------------------------------------------------
2. BASIC ARCHITECTURE (MENTAL MODEL)
---------------------------------------------------------

        Leader A  ←→  Leader B  ←→  Leader C

- Each leader:
    → Accepts writes
    → Sends updates to others

Flow:
1. User writes to nearest leader
2. That leader updates local DB
3. Sends changes to other leaders
4. Others apply updates

---------------------------------------------------------
3. WHY DO WE NEED MULTI-LEADER?
---------------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
EXAMPLE: GLOBAL APPLICATION
-------------------------------------

- Users worldwide
- Writing to single leader = high latency

Solution:
- Local leaders in each region

-------------------------------------
BENEFITS
-------------------------------------

1. LOW LATENCY WRITES
- Write to nearest leader

2. HIGH AVAILABILITY
- If one leader fails → others still work

3. OFFLINE SUPPORT
- Example: mobile apps syncing later

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF (VERY IMPORTANT 🔥)
-------------------------------------

- CONFLICTS 💥
- Complexity increases massively
- Hard to maintain consistency

---------------------------------------------------------
4. CORE PROBLEM: WRITE CONFLICTS
---------------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

User A (India):
    sets name = "Anubhav"

User B (US):
    sets name = "Anu"

Both updates happen at same time

Now:
→ Which one is correct?

-------------------------------------
THIS IS THE MAIN CHALLENGE
-------------------------------------

Single leader → no conflict  
Multi-leader → conflicts inevitable

---------------------------------------------------------
5. TOPOLOGIES (VERY IMPORTANT 🔥)
---------------------------------------------------------

Topology = How leaders are connected

=========================================================
5.1 CIRCULAR (RING) TOPOLOGY
=========================================================

-------------------------------------
STRUCTURE
-------------------------------------

A → B → C → A

Each node sends updates to ONE neighbor

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE ANALOGY
-------------------------------------

Like passing a message in a circle

-------------------------------------
HOW IT WORKS
-------------------------------------

- A sends to B
- B sends to C
- C sends to A

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

If ONE node fails:
→ Chain breaks

Example:
A → B (fails) → C

Now:
- A cannot reach C

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ Simple
- Fragile
- High latency (updates travel long path)

=========================================================
5.2 STAR TOPOLOGY
=========================================================

-------------------------------------
STRUCTURE
-------------------------------------

        Central Node
        /    |    \
       A     B     C

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE ANALOGY
-------------------------------------

Like a hub airport

-------------------------------------
HOW IT WORKS
-------------------------------------

- All leaders sync via central node

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

Central node fails:
→ Entire replication stops

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ Easy coordination
- Single point of failure

=========================================================
5.3 ALL-TO-ALL TOPOLOGY (MOST IMPORTANT 🔥🔥)
=========================================================

-------------------------------------
STRUCTURE
-------------------------------------

A ↔ B
A ↔ C
B ↔ C

Each node connected to EVERY other node

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

Google Docs / distributed systems

-------------------------------------
HOW IT WORKS
-------------------------------------

- Every leader sends updates to ALL others

-------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES
-------------------------------------

+ Fast propagation
+ No single failure point

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM (BIG ONE 🔥)
-------------------------------------

Updates can arrive in DIFFERENT ORDER

-------------------------------------
EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

Leader A:
    update1

Leader B:
    update2

At node C:
    may receive update2 before update1

→ Order mismatch

-------------------------------------
THIS LEADS TO:
-------------------------------------

- Inconsistency
- Conflicts

---------------------------------------------------------
6. MODIFYING THE REPLICATION LOG
---------------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

- Logs may arrive out of order
- Need to resolve conflicts

-------------------------------------
SOLUTION IDEA
-------------------------------------

Modify logs BEFORE applying

-------------------------------------
HOW?
-------------------------------------

- Detect conflicts
- Transform operations

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

Google Docs:
- Two users edit same line
→ System merges intelligently

-------------------------------------
TECHNIQUES
-------------------------------------

1. Operation Transformation
2. Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ Smart conflict resolution
- Complex logic
- Hard to implement

---------------------------------------------------------
7. LAST WRITE WINS (LWW)
---------------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
REAL LIFE EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------

User A:
    sets status = "Online"

User B:
    sets status = "Busy"

System picks:
→ whichever came LAST

-------------------------------------
DEFINITION
-------------------------------------

Latest write overwrites previous ones

-------------------------------------
HOW IT WORKS
-------------------------------------

- Each write has timestamp
- Compare timestamps
- Keep latest

-------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES
-------------------------------------

+ Simple
+ Fast

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

DATA LOSS ❌

Example:
- Important update overwritten

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ Easy
- Risky (loses data)

---------------------------------------------------------
8. TIMESTAMPS (SENDER vs RECEIVER)
---------------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
8.1 SENDER TIMESTAMP
-------------------------------------

- Timestamp assigned at source

Example:
A writes at time=10

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

Clock differences

Example:
A clock = 10
B clock = 8

→ Wrong ordering

-------------------------------------

8.2 RECEIVER TIMESTAMP
-------------------------------------

- Timestamp assigned when received

-------------------------------------
PROBLEM
-------------------------------------

Network delay affects order

-------------------------------------

-------------------------------------
REAL ISSUE
-------------------------------------

No global clock ❌

-------------------------------------
SOLUTION IDEAS
-------------------------------------

- Logical clocks
- Vector clocks (advanced)

-------------------------------------
TRADE-OFF
-------------------------------------

+ Helps ordering
- Not perfect
- Needs extra metadata

---------------------------------------------------------
9. BIG PICTURE CONNECTION 🔥
---------------------------------------------------------

Multi-Leader gives:
+ Availability
+ Low latency

But introduces:
- Conflicts
- Ordering issues
- Complexity

So we need:
- Conflict resolution (LWW, CRDTs)
- Ordering (timestamps)
- Smart topologies

---------------------------------------------------------
10. INTERVIEW GOLD POINTS ⭐
---------------------------------------------------------

If asked:
"Why not multi-leader everywhere?"

Answer:

Because:
- Conflict resolution is hard
- Data consistency becomes complex

Use multi-leader when:
- Geo-distributed writes needed
- Offline support required

Avoid when:
- Strong consistency needed

---------------------------------------------------------
11. FINAL INTUITION
---------------------------------------------------------

Single Leader:
→ Safe but slow

Multi-Leader:
→ Fast but chaotic

Distributed systems rule:
More freedom → more complexity

